# The composition rule, computed and set beside what was filed. One row per (composed layer,
# quantity) that owes the equality, carrying the sum of the converted parts, the elimination filed
# against that quantity, the result, the figure the document states, and whether the two agree.
#
#     x_composed = F Φ x_parts − e_x
#
# Per quantity
#   Demand, nameplate and draw each owe the sum separately, and an elimination names exactly one
#   of them, so the sum is folds/part_sums.sqlc's, one row per (composition, composed layer,
#   quantity), and the owed pair, the filed figure and the elimination each meet it one row per
#   key. `sum_low`, `sum_mode` and `sum_high` are `F Φ x` before the elimination, which is the
#   product examples/matrices/main.rs recomputes as a matrix product.
#
# Component-wise, unless that inverts
#   The elimination is subtracted bound by bound: the overlap is part of what the sum counts, so
#   at the sum's low corner it is at its own low. That pairing can invert. Point parts that sum to
#   20 with an elimination of `[2, 3, 5]` give `[18, 17, 15]`, which is not a claim. There the
#   sum has no width for the overlap to move with, so the only ordered reading is the crossed one,
#   `[Σ low - e_high, Σ mode - e_mode, Σ high - e_low]`, `[15, 17, 18]`, and that is the figure a
#   correct filing states. eliminations/paired.sqlc decides which reading holds and reads each
#   elimination at its corners, and `crossed` says which a row took. Proof:
#   `src/proofs/README.md`, entry `elimination_componentwise`.
#
# The elimination is the term that is easy to forget
#   Forgetting it is the ordinary way to get this arithmetic wrong. Two parts that both count the
#   same shared machine sum to more machine than exists; `e` is what the composer says was counted
#   twice. Dropping it does not produce an obviously silly number: it produces a slightly large
#   one, which is worse.
#
# Missing rows are not failures
#   The rows that are not here are not failures. A quantity suspended on any ground is absent
#   rather than counted as a pass, so `agrees` is a verdict over documents that claimed something.
#   See composition/owed_equality.sqlc.
#
# It spans two documents
#   That is why no grammar can state it. The parts are in one filing and the composed figure is in
#   another; XSD 1.0 cannot follow the reference, let alone add up what is on the other end.
-- folds/part_sums.sqlc against the composed layer's own figure, less eliminations/paired.sqlc at its corners.
SELECT x.composition, x.composed_layer, x.quantity,
       x.sum_low, x.sum_mode, x.sum_high, x.crossed,
       x.sum_low  - x.e_at_low  AS computed_low,
       x.sum_mode - x.e_mode    AS computed_mode,
       x.sum_high - x.e_at_high AS computed_high,
       x.filed_low, x.filed_mode, x.filed_high,
       (x.sum_low  - x.e_at_low  = x.filed_low
    AND x.sum_mode - x.e_mode    = x.filed_mode
    AND x.sum_high - x.e_at_high = x.filed_high) AS agrees
FROM (
    SELECT s.composition, s.composed_layer, s.quantity,
           s.sum_low, s.sum_mode, s.sum_high,
           coalesce(e.crossed, false) AS crossed,
           coalesce(e.at_low,  0) AS e_at_low,
           coalesce(e.mode,    0) AS e_mode,
           coalesce(e.at_high, 0) AS e_at_high,
           d.low  AS filed_low,
           d.mode AS filed_mode,
           d.high AS filed_high
    FROM      (
        :compose(folds/part_sums.sqlc)
    ) s
    JOIN      (
        :compose(composition/owed_equality.sqlc)
    ) o
           ON o.filing = s.composition AND o.layer = s.composed_layer
          AND o.quantity = s.quantity
    JOIN      (
        :compose(layers/summed_quantities.sqlc)
    ) d
           ON d.filing = s.composition AND d.layer = s.composed_layer
          AND d.quantity = s.quantity
    LEFT JOIN (
        :compose(eliminations/paired.sqlc)
    ) e
           ON e.composition = s.composition
          AND e.composed_layer = s.composed_layer
          AND e.quantity = s.quantity
) x
